    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d919">

                <pb facs="1732" n="1692" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1692"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.74/11–1154: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 919<lb/>The Secretary of State to the Embassy in Egypt <anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d919-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1954-11-13T12:55:00-05:00">November 13, 1954—12:55 p.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <p rendition="#flush">Secret</p>
                <p>750. Embtel 641.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d919-f2"/> Suggest you inform Fawzi
                    Department pleased know his desire for closer cooperation and glad consider
                    practical <pb facs="1733" n="1693" xml:id="pg1693"/>steps clearing away
                    underbrush of immediate problems concentrating on:</p>
                <p>1. Gaza situation as previously discussed;</p>
                <p>2. Freedom of transit Suez Canal. US encouraged by Azmi’s statement in Security
                    Council Oct 14 that since Mar 1954 Egypt has refrained from interference
                    shipping through Canal to or from Israel.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d919-f3"/> We
                    wish cooperate GOE find formula enabling it remove remaining restrictions and
                    avoiding continued public dispute. Recommend to GOE consideration of a Security
                    Council resolution requesting advisory opinion on freedom of transit issue from
                    International Court Justice in whose legal decisions world public has full
                    confidence. Egypt itself might wish make proposal in Security Council and
                    reiterate statement re its present policy. Alternatively some member Security
                    Council could introduce resolution. FYI: See Deptel 241 to USUN rptd Cairo 698
                    Tel Aviv 247<anchor n="4" xml:id="d919-f4"/> for proposed text resolution, which
                    should not be disclosed to Egyptians this time. End FYI.</p>
                <p>Security Council could then note Egyptian statement on non-interference and refer
                    all legal points involved to ICJ. FYI: We plan discuss this point orally with
                    Hussein here after approach Cairo. End FYI.</p>
                <p>3. Jerusalem. Urge again Egypt seek moderation in Arab attitude and emphasize US
                    position in support of UN’s primary role in determining future status remains
                    unchanged.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Dulles </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d919-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d919-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Tel Aviv; sent by pouch to London and New York.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d919-f2" n="2" xml:id="d919-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 641 from Cairo, Nov. 11, Caffery reported on a conversation that
                    morning with Fawzi, in the course of which the Egyptian Foreign Minister
                    expressed an interest in practical steps that might be taken with regard to
                    Palestine, both at present and in the future, including more cooperation and
                    exchange of good will. (611.74/11–1154)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d919-f3" n="3" xml:id="d919-f3-content">
                <p>See U.N. doc. S/PV.682.</p>
            </note>
